   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the judiciary law, in relation to nonpartisan quadrennial review of the adequacy of judgeships of the unified court system",
    "basePrintNo": "S7505",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7505-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §212, Judy L",
    "lawSection": "Judiciary Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7505\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the judiciary law, in relation to nonpartisan\nquadrennial review of the adequacy of judgeships of the unified court\nsystem\n\n\n PURPOSE :\nDirects the Office of Court Administration to issue a report every\nfour years regarding the adequacy and allocation of judgeships in and\namong the state-paid trial courts of the state.\n\n SUMMARY :\nAmends Judiciary Law section 212 to require the Office of Court\nAdministration to transmit to the Legislature and governor, every four\nyears starting November 1 after enactment, a report on the adequacy\nand allocation of judgeships among the nine state-paid trial courts.\nSuch reports and their recommendations must be without regard to\npartisanship but based on the administration of justice in and among\nthe courts and broader justice system, taking into consideration\ncurrent and projected dockets.  Reports would include the objective\nbasis for any recommendations.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nCurrent law provides no mechanism by which the legislative and\nexecutive branches can receive, on a predictable basis, objective\nassessments of the adequacy and allocation of judgeships in state-paid\ncourts relative to dockets and other incidents of the administration\nof justice. This gap in the law governing the Judiciary impairs the\neffective operation of the courts and frustrates efforts to redress\ncalendar congestion that delays proceedings and directly impacts\nlitigants and their families and communities.\n\nDockets among New York's state-paid courts are nearing five million\nnew filings annually, dwarfing all federal courts combined and most\nother state judiciaries. Explosive growth in New York dockets,\ncombined with new procedures for many case types, impose mounting\nburdens on many courts, litigants and allied justice agencies\nstatewide. The limited issue this bill seeks to address is how to\nmeasure the judgeship needs, if any, arising from these trends.\n\nOn its own initiative over the last several decades, the Judiciary\nestablished an informal periodic process to propose adjustments in and\namong City Courts outside New York City.  This process has worked to\nthe benefit of the institutional Judiciary, the legislative and\nexecutive branches, the affected courts and the regions they serve -\nby providing a regular timetable and basis for this inquiry. Under\nthis system, the Legislature receives a periodic assessment, based on\ndockets and projections in each of the 62 City Courts, as well as\nqualitative reports on emerging trends. Using these data and the\ntimetable's regularity, OCA makes a comprehensive proposal to the\nLegislature, which then has full discretion to consider and adjust\nThis process has streamlined what once was a haphazard approach that\nleft some City Courts and their communities without needed resources\nand others with extra capacity. With benefit of this process, City\nCourts have evolved commensurate with their duties, with system-wide\nefficiencies and justice benefits for affected cities, counties and\ntheir civil and criminal justice systems. Likewise, the Legislature -\nwhile always free to make mid-cycle adjustments - has streamlined a\nprocess that otherwise would invite stand alone bills for each of 62\nseparate courts.\n\nBy contrast, for the other eight state-paid trial courts - Supreme\nCourt, Family Court, County Court, Surrogate's Court, Court of Claims,\nNew York City Civil Court, New York City Criminal Court, and District\nCourts on Long Island - there is no periodic process to measure\njudgeship needs and propose adjustments. Adjustments in these courts\ngenerally remain haphazard in and among the 62 counties of the state,\nand some courts have grown critically backlogged. For instance, Family\nCourt has not had a judgeship package focused on that essential\ntribunal's needs since the Court's creation in 1962.  As of the date\nthis bill first was proposed, there wasn't a single new Family Court\njudgeship created in New York City for over 20 years, even though\ndockets and the burdens of Family Court adjudications have ballooned.\nDockets in other courts and venues also have become critically high\nrelative to judgeships.\n\nThis bill's purpose is to provide for other state-paid courts the\nregular, streamlined and objective process on which the Legislature\nhas come to rely for City Courts. This bill would provide the\nfirst-ever snapshot of potential judgeship needs for all state-paid\ncourts, as a system, relative to dockets and the administration of\njustice. As demonstrated above, history shows that absent such a\nsystematic approach, the public and their representatives cannot have\nfull confidence that the administration of justice properly is served\nin and among the courts of this state.\n\nAccordingly, this measure would amend Judiciary Law section 212 to\ndirect the Office of Court Administration to transmit a quadrennial\nreport on the sufficiency of judgeships in and among the state-paid\ncourts, based on current and projected dockets and other\nadministration-of-justice factors. The amendment would require that\nsuch reports be entirely without regard to partisanship and based on\nobjective data, which would be submitted as part of the report to\ninform the discretion of the Legislature and governor.\n\nNothing in this bill would intrude on the constitutional prerogatives\nof the legislative and executive branches, which would retain their\ndiscretion in relation to judgeships consistent with Article VI of the\nConstitution. This bill would, however, create a first-ever process\nfor the legislative and executive branches to examine the judgeship\nneeds of the system as a system, to promote efficiency and targeted\nresponse to docket trends. The result would be a tailored approach\nthat allows regular improvements for the benefit of litigants, their\nfamilies and communities, civil and criminal justice agencies, and the\ncause of justice itself.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\nNone.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nNone. New bill.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act would take effect immediately.\n",
